ref('belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite','Belavin, A. A. and Polyakov, A. M. and Zamolodchikov, A. B.','Infinite conformal symmetry in two-dimensional quantum field theory',1984,'https://qft.itp.ac.ru/BPZ.pdf',journal='Nuclear Physics B',volume=241,pages='333--380',doi='10.1016/0550-3213(84)90052-X',annote='Full original paper opened. Sections 2--5, especially (2.18)--(2.27), (3.9)--(3.10), (4.5)--(4.18), and (5.1)--(5.8) read. Received 22 November 1983; published 1984. Earlier vertex-operator antecedents are credited by this paper, not independently dated here.')
ref('guillarmoukupiainenrhodesvargas2024bootstrap','Guillarmou, Colin and Kupiainen, Antti and Rhodes, R{\\\'e}mi and Vargas, Vincent','Conformal bootstrap in {Liouville} theory',2024,'https://arxiv.org/pdf/2005.11530',journal='Acta Mathematica',volume=233,number=1,pages='33--194',doi='10.4310/ACTA.2024.v233.n1.a2',annote='Full arXiv v2 primary preprint read: Theorem 1.1, Corollary 1.2, Sections 2.3--2.6, Theorem 6.25, and Section 8. First arXiv 23 May 2020, revision 11 November 2020 confirmed on arXiv. Published 2024 metadata separately checked in the author institutional record by the collaborating shard; direct publisher DOI inaccessible here. Regime gamma in (0,2); not arbitrary complex or timelike coupling.')
talk(573,'1982-11-01','A. B. Zamolodchikov','P001057-P001061','Primary fields, Virasoro degeneracy, and the Liouville spectrum',r'The entry begins on P001058 and ends on P001059; the broader catalogue range includes the preceding surface lecture and following Beilinson record. Zamolodchikov asks how to compute primary-field matrix elements and which highest-weight modules have null states; Neretin supplies the reducibility criterion. The second half returns to the independent-metric string integral, the Liouville action, a cylinder evolution kernel and a conjectural Virasoro description of the spectrum. These are distinct questions: BPZ develops the correlator program in 1984, whereas a much later spectral theorem gives a rigorous real-coupling Liouville realization. Several notebook formulas require consistent mode conventions or substantive emendations.')
add(573,'The Virasoro bracket and its missing cubic central factor','P001058','definition',r'\([L_n,L_m]=(m-n)L_{n+m}+(c/12)\delta_{n+m,0}\mathbf1\). The scan confirms that no cubic mode factor is written.',r'Use one convention throughout: \([L_n,L_m]=(n-m)L_{n+m}+\frac{c}{12}(n^3-n)\delta_{n+m,0}\mathbf1\), with \(L_n\) for \(n>0\) annihilating a highest-weight vector. Reversing or negating generators requires corresponding changes in weights, central terms and field transformations. The constant delta term in the notebook is not antisymmetric, already giving \([L_0,L_0]=c\mathbf1/12\).',r'The central extension predates the talk: \citet[Theorem~1]{gelfandfuchs1968circle}. The convention matching the recorded primary commutator is printed in \citet[equation~(2.21)]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite}.',('gelfandfuchs1968circle','belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite'),literal='false as written',verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='The standard highest-weight convention is fixed for this whole record; the missing polynomial is an emendation.')
add(573,'A cyclic highest-weight representation and its Verma cover','P001058','construction',r'\(L_n|0\rangle=0\) for \(n>0\), \(L_0|0\rangle=\Delta|0\rangle\), and all vectors are generated by products of negative modes.',r'For fixed \(c,h\), the induced Virasoro Verma module \(M(c,h)\) has a basis \(L_{-n_1}\cdots L_{-n_k}|h\rangle\), \(n_1\ge\cdots\ge n_k\ge1\), and level-\(N\) dimension \(p(N)\). Every cyclic highest-weight representation with these parameters is its quotient. The notebook generation assertion alone does not say whether the quotient is already irreducible. A general highest-weight vector is not the conformally invariant vacuum: the latter also has \(h=0\) and \(L_{-1}|0\rangle=0\).',r'The Verma construction and determinant were established before November 1982; see \citet[Sections~3--4]{feiginfuchs1982invariant}. The vacuum distinction is explicit in \citet[equations~(2.23)--(2.27)]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite}.',('feiginfuchs1982invariant','belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite'),verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='Ordered PBW monomials, the universal quotient and distinct notation h for the state weight are supplied.')
add(573,'Primary covariance does not uniquely define the field','P001058','claim',r'\([L_n,\varphi(z)]=z^{n+1}\varphi^{\prime}(z)+(n+1)\Delta z^n\varphi(z)\), followed by \ru{Он опрд. этой ф-лой однозначно}.',r'This commutator defines the covariance of a primary field of weight \(\delta\), where \(\delta\) need not equal the highest weight \(h\) of its source module. It does not determine an operator uniquely: multiplication by any scalar preserves the equation, and module channels and multiplicities must be specified. For fixed generic source and target Verma modules, formal matrix elements may be recursively fixed after a compatible leading matrix element has been chosen.',r'\citet[equation~(3.9) and its following sentence]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite} identifies the law with earlier dual-model vertex operators; its historical antecedent is verified indirectly. The failure of literal uniqueness follows directly by rescaling.',('belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite',),literal='false as written',verification='indirect',match='exact',reconstruction='The field weight, source and target data and normalization are separated. No unrestricted existence or uniqueness of intertwiners is asserted.',status='primary covariance is standard, but the printed uniqueness claim lacks essential data.')
add(573,'Computing primary correlators and the role of null states','P001058','question',r'The first question asks how to calculate \(\langle0|\varphi(z_1)\cdots\varphi(z_n)|0\rangle\).',r'For specified Virasoro modules, primary weights, intertwining channels and leading three-point matrix elements, commute modes to determine descendant matrix elements and conformal-block coefficients. In a vacuum correlator, global Ward identities constrain the position dependence; three-point coefficients and four-point cross-ratio dependence require dynamical data. If a singular vector vanishes in a chosen irreducible quotient, its decoupling gives a differential equation for correlators. Without those extra choices the displayed expectation has no unique numerical answer.',r'This is a close precursor of \citet[Sections~3--5]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite}: the paper separates conformal blocks from structure constants and derives differential equations for degenerate fields. Received on 22 November 1983 and published in 1984, it is a later publication by the speaker, not a theorem already published in November 1982.',('belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite',),cl='A',verification='read the result',match='partial',problem='GP-1982-08',reconstruction='The additional channel and structure-constant data make the computational question definite. A classification of every conformal field theory is not inserted into the notes.',status='the Ward-identity, block and null-state program is established; the data in the notebook do not select a unique full conformal field theory.')
add(573,'The contravariant Gram form and positivity','P001058','construction',r'\(L_{-n}^{+}=L_n\) and \(\langle0|0\rangle=1\) are said to recover the scalar product.',r'For real \(c,h\), these conditions determine the contravariant Hermitian form on the Verma module by moving positive modes to the right; for complex parameters use the algebraic bilinear form with the standard antiautomorphism. The form can be degenerate or indefinite. For example \(\|L_{-1}|h\rangle\|^2=2h\), so positivity does not follow for arbitrary \(h\). The radical is removed when forming the irreducible highest-weight quotient.',r'\citet[Section~3 and Theorem~3.1]{feiginfuchs1982invariant} gives the determinant of this form before the talk. The displayed level-one norm is a direct check \ours.',('feiginfuchs1982invariant',),verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='Hermitian versus bilinear conventions and the absence of automatic positivity are made explicit.')
add(573,'Neretin on reducibility and the first singular level','P001058','question',r'Neretin gives a two-factor polynomial with \(-\Delta\) in both factors, then says degeneration occurs at level \(\alpha\beta\), for positive integers \(\alpha,\beta\).',r'With the standard generators \(\ell_n=-L_n\) for the recorded reverse Witt bracket, their highest weight is \(h=-\Delta\). Put \(c=13-6(t+t^{-1})\), \(t\ne0\), using the corresponding central normalization. Reducibility means \(h=((rt-s)^2-(t-1)^2)/(4t)\) for some \(r,s\ge1\). The first singular level is the least such \(rs\). Thus the recorded \(-\Delta\) is exactly \(h\), and the symmetric two-factor polynomial has the correct Kac form. If one instead repairs the opening bracket to the standard convention while keeping \(h=\Delta\), the same expression must be translated coherently. The level-two Gram determinant is \(2h[16h^2+(2c-10)h+c]\), so at \(c=0\) the nonzero level-two weight is \(h=5/8\), corresponding to the recorded \(\Delta=-5/8\).',r'This repeats 1982-566.7 and GP-1982-03. \citet[Theorem~3.1 and Section~4]{feiginfuchs1982invariant} had already proved the criterion. The low-level check \oursComputed verifies the convention conversion. The polynomial is not itself a new error; the incompatible surrounding conventions must be repaired as a collection.',('feiginfuchs1982invariant',),literal='correct with missing hypotheses',verification='read the result',match='exact',problem='GP-1982-03',speaker='Yu. A. Neretin',reconstruction='The recorded polynomial is preserved under h = minus Delta; minima over coincident degeneracy conditions qualify the first-level assertion.')
add(573,'Independent embeddings and worldsheet metrics','P001058','construction',r'Equation (1) integrates independent \(\vec x(\xi)\) and \(g_{ab}(\xi)\) with the Dirichlet action and a cosmological area term; the margin emphasizes that the metric is not induced.',r'For a fixed smooth worldsheet \(\Sigma\), the Polyakov functional is \(S[X,g]=\frac12\int_\Sigma\sqrt g\,g^{ab}\partial_aX\cdot\partial_bX+\mu_0^2\int_\Sigma\sqrt g\). It treats the embedding and positive metric as independent variables. A quantum partition function additionally needs a topology, boundary conditions, regularization, target zero-mode normalization and a quotient by diffeomorphisms. The formal symbol is not by itself a finite measure.',r'The independent-metric formulation is in \citet[equations~(5)--(11)]{polyakov1981quantum}, published in July 1981. It recurs from records 567 and 569.',('polyakov1981quantum',),verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='Fixed topology and the missing analytic data are explicit, without changing the recorded formal action.')
add(573,'Diffeomorphism quotient and conformal representatives','P001058','method',r'\(\delta g_{ab}=\nabla_a\varepsilon_b+\nabla_b\varepsilon_a\); the volume is factored by this group, and a representative \(g_{ab}=\rho\delta_{ab}\) is chosen.',r'Infinitesimal diffeomorphisms have the displayed Lie derivative. Locally a two-dimensional metric has isothermal coordinates. Globally on a fixed surface one instead writes \(g=e^\varphi\widehat g(m)\), allowing conformal moduli \(m\), and divides residual automorphisms. A fixed global Euclidean metric is unavailable on a general closed surface. For the ordinary geometric quotient, integration is over orbits as points of the quotient, not along their unreduced volumes.',r'\citet[equations~(9)--(14)]{polyakov1981quantum} is the prior formal gauge-fixing source. The local/global distinction also qualifies the repeated construction in 1982-569.9.',('polyakov1981quantum',),verification='read the result',match='partial',reconstruction='Moduli, reference metric and residual stabilizers are restored; the flat formula is interpreted locally.')
add(573,'Scalar and ghost determinants after gauge fixing','P001058-P001059','method',r'Equation (2) contains \([\det\Delta_g]^{-D/2}[\det\mathcal L]^{1/2}\), while (3) writes an extra upper \(a\) on the first covariant derivative. The later definition makes \(\Delta_g=\operatorname{div}\operatorname{grad}\).',r'For positive Euclidean metric the Gaussian matter determinant is \((\det{}^{\prime}(-\Delta_g))^{-D/2}\), with zero modes treated separately. Put \((P\varepsilon)_{ab}=\nabla_a\varepsilon_b+\nabla_b\varepsilon_a-g_{ab}\nabla^c\varepsilon_c\). With the standard \(L^2\) metrics, the notebook operator after removing the extra index is \(\mathcal L=-\frac12P^*P\). The positive ghost determinant is therefore expressed through \((\det{}^{\prime}P^*P)^{1/2}\), with convention-dependent constants, conformal Killing modes and moduli factors. Equation (2) also suppresses the cosmological weight.',r'\citet[equations~(12)--(16)]{polyakov1981quantum} gives the formal determinant mechanism. Integration by parts fixes the adjoint sign \ours; it is not a claim that the unregularized determinants exist.',('polyakov1981quantum',),literal='false as written',verification='read the result',match='partial',reconstruction='Positive operator, primed determinants, the covector operator and missing factors are specified; the doubtful upper index remains a palaeographic ambiguity.')
add(573,'The induced Liouville action and its quantum measure','P001059','construction',r'For \(D<26\), equation (4) gives a formal weight with coefficient \((26-D)/(48\pi)\), kinetic term \(\frac12(\partial\varphi)^2\), potential \(m_0^2e^\varphi\), and \(\rho=e^\varphi\).',r'This is the flat-reference formal Weyl-anomaly action. On a curved reference surface one needs a background-curvature term and a specified renormalized field measure. In the real probabilistic Liouville theory, \(0<\gamma<2\), \(\mu>0\), \(Q=2/\gamma+\gamma/2\), the exponential interaction is Gaussian-multiplicative-chaos renormalized; sphere insertions satisfy Seiberg bounds. The bare coefficient alone is not a rigorous measure definition and does not identify every \(D<26\) theory with this regime.',r'The anomaly action was derived in \citet[equations~(15)--(18)]{polyakov1981quantum}. \citet[Theorems~3.2 and 3.11]{davidkupiainenrhodesvargas2016liouville}, first circulated in October 2014 and published in 2016, constructs a specified real-coupling sphere theory. These are different levels of assertion.',('polyakov1981quantum','davidkupiainenrhodesvargas2016liouville'),verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='Modern parameters, renormalization and curvature terms are qualifications, not formulas attributed to the 1982 notes.')
add(573,'The claim that one integral would settle the string problem','P001059','remark',r'\ru{Задача о струне будет решена, если вычислить этот интеграл}.',r'The sentence identifies the Liouville integral as a central remaining calculation, but it does not specify a worldsheet topology, observables, boundary conditions, a genus sum or the sense in which a string theory would be solved. It is not a definite assertion that evaluating one scalar partition function determines every string amplitude.',r'The modern sphere construction \citep{davidkupiainenrhodesvargas2016liouville} and bootstrap theorem \citep[Theorem~1.1]{guillarmoukupiainenrhodesvargas2024bootstrap} answer well-specified Liouville questions. They cannot resolve the unqualified phrase as a single mathematical problem.',('davidkupiainenrhodesvargas2016liouville','guillarmoukupiainenrhodesvargas2024bootstrap'),cl='F',literal='not determinable',verification='read the result',match='thematic only',confidence='medium',status='the broad string-theory assertion remains underdetermined; no new open problem is manufactured from it.')
add(573,'Critical dimension and the remaining worldsheet integrations','P001059','claim',r'For \(D=26\), the answer is said to equal the flat-metric answer, and dual models are called exact.',r'For \(D\) free bosons, matter and reparametrization ghosts have total central charge \(D-26\), so their local Weyl anomalies cancel at \(D=26\). This permits elimination of the Weyl factor in the critical theory, subject to the cosmological and regularization prescriptions. It does not remove moduli, ghost zero modes, vertex positions, boundary data or genus dependence; nor can every worldsheet be represented by one global flat metric.',r'This is the local anomaly-cancellation statement of \citet[equations~(15)--(18)]{polyakov1981quantum}. Reading the flat-metric shortcut as a global equality loses required integrations.',('polyakov1981quantum',),literal='false as written',verification='read the result',match='partial',reconstruction='The exact local cancellation is separated from the unqualified global assertion.')
add(573,'Cylinder evolution and the spectral representation','P001059','method',r'A drawing joins contours \(c_1,c_2\); equation (5) is \(\sum_nG(c_1,n)G(c_2,n)e^{-E_nt}\), annotated \(t\to\infty\).',r'For a specified self-adjoint Hamiltonian bounded below and suitable boundary vectors, \(Z(c_1,c_2;t)=\langle c_2,e^{-tH}c_1\rangle=\int e^{-tE}\,d\langle c_2,\Pi_Ec_1\rangle\). A sum is valid for a discrete orthonormal eigenbasis, with the appropriate dual or complex-conjugate amplitude. Large time isolates a lowest eigenstate only if one exists with nonzero overlap; at a continuous threshold one must retain a spectral integral and its threshold asymptotics.',r'The spectral-theorem interpretation is old; the Liouville realization in \citet[Sections~2.3--2.4 and 6]{guillarmoukupiainenrhodesvargas2024bootstrap} exhibits continuous momentum. The notebook cylinder is a propagation diagram, not evidence of a discrete Liouville spectrum.',('guillarmoukupiainenrhodesvargas2024bootstrap',),cl='B',verification='read the result',match='partial',reconstruction='A Hilbert space, boundary vectors and spectral measure replace schematic amplitudes; no large-time gap is assumed.')
add(573,'Liouville stationary states as Virasoro representations','P001059','conjecture',r'After the Liouville Lagrangian (6), the hypothesis says that its stationary spectrum is related to the \(L_n\) system on the preceding page.',r'A precise modern realization uses \(0<\gamma<2\), \(\mu>0\), \(Q=2/\gamma+\gamma/2\), and \(c_L=1+6Q^2>25\). Generalized primary states are labelled by \(\alpha=Q+iP\), \(P>0\), with left and right weights \((Q^2+P^2)/4\); their descendants have oscillator levels. In the Hamiltonian normalization of the cited preprint the energies are \((Q^2+P^2)/2+N+\bar N\), and the spectral resolution integrates over \(P\), rather than summing only over a discrete set. This realizes the recorded representation-theoretic relation in an explicitly constructed quantum theory.',r'\citet[Theorem~6.25, Sections~2.4--2.6 and 8]{guillarmoukupiainenrhodesvargas2024bootstrap} supplies the complete spectral resolution and its conformal-block application. The first preprint was 23 May 2020, the version covering \(0<\gamma<2\) is dated 11 November 2020, and publication is 2024. The bare 1982 action must first be renormalized and normalized as that theory.',('guillarmoukupiainenrhodesvargas2024bootstrap',),cl='B',verification='read the result',match='partial',problem='GP-1982-09',reconstruction='The note-supported relation is retained separately from this precise real-coupling implementation. No assertion is made about timelike coupling or all target dimensions.',status='the stated real-coupling Liouville spectral relation is rigorously established; this is not a claim that the whole string integral is evaluated.')
add(573,'The improved classical Liouville stress tensor','P001059','claim',r'The purported conserved traceless tensor ends with \(\alpha\varphi_{ab}-\beta\delta_{ab}(\partial\varphi)^2\); it has no potential or Laplacian improvement term.',r'For \(S=\int[\frac12|\nabla\varphi|^2+V(\varphi)]\), \(V=m_0^2e^\varphi\), the equation is \(\Delta\varphi=V\). One consistent tensor is \[T_{ab}=\varphi_a\varphi_b-\delta_{ab}\bigl(\tfrac12|\nabla\varphi|^2+V\bigr)+2(\delta_{ab}\Delta\varphi-\varphi_{ab}).\] Its divergence is \((\Delta\varphi-V)\varphi_b\), and its trace is \(2(\Delta\varphi-V)\), so both vanish on shell. The printed ansatz cannot achieve both for arbitrary Liouville solutions by choosing constants \(\alpha,\beta\).',r'This is an elementary classical check \ours of the notebook action, within the Liouville setting of \citet{polyakov1981quantum}; it is not a quantum stress-tensor construction. Differentiation and a separate trace calculation provide independent checks.',('polyakov1981quantum',),literal='false as written',verification='read the result',match='partial',reconstruction='The complete improved tensor is an editorial emendation; the notebook gives only a malformed ansatz.')
add(573,'Holomorphic stress components and the sign of the improvement','P001059','claim',r'\(T=\varphi_z^2+2\varphi_{zz}\), \(\bar T=\varphi_{\bar z}^2+2\varphi_{\bar z\bar z}\), followed by \(\partial_{\bar z}T=0\) and a second \(\partial_zT=0\) without a bar.',r'With the recorded \(e^\varphi\) potential, \(\varphi_{z\bar z}=m_0^2e^\varphi/4\), so \[T=\varphi_z^2-2\varphi_{zz},\qquad \bar T=\varphi_{\bar z}^2-2\varphi_{\bar z\bar z}\] obey \(\partial_{\bar z}T=\partial_z\bar T=0\). The printed plus sign instead gives \(\partial_{\bar z}T=m_0^2e^\varphi\varphi_z\), generally nonzero. An overall normalization or simultaneous \(\varphi\mapsto-\varphi\) with a changed action is possible, but cannot preserve all printed formulas.',r'The chain-rule computation \ours directly checks conservation against equation (6). The general holomorphic stress-tensor mechanism is explained in \citet[Section~2]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite}.',('belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite',),literal='false as written',verification='read the result',match='partial',reconstruction='The action fixes the relative sign; the missing bar is a separate emendation confirmed by the scan.')
add(573,'Recovering Virasoro modes from the stress field','P001059','construction',r'The final formula is \(T(z)=\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}z^{n-2}L_n\), followed by the assertion that this is where the \(L_n\) come from.',r'In the highest-weight convention used above, \[L_n=\frac{1}{2\pi i}\oint z^{n+1}T(z)\,dz,\qquad T(z)=\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}L_nz^{-n-2}.\] The notebook series can be used only after relabelling its coefficient \(L_n\) as the standard \(L_{-n}\) and adjusting all adjoining conventions. Classically such modes have Poisson brackets; quantum Virasoro commutators and their central term require a quantum stress field with its operator product and normal ordering, not a Laurent expansion alone.',r'\citet[equations~(2.18)--(2.21)]{belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite} makes the mode construction explicit; the Virasoro algebra itself is earlier. The distinction between extraction and quantization prevents the final sentence from being read as a proof of the spectral conjecture.',('belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite','gelfandfuchs1968circle'),literal='false as written',verification='read the result',match='exact',reconstruction='The mode relabelling is fixed relative to the primary commutator and highest-weight conditions, and classical versus quantum operations are separated.')

next(x for x in problems if x['id']=='GP-1982-03')['occurrences'].append('1982-573.6')
problems.append(dict(id='GP-1982-08',title='Primary correlators, conformal blocks and null-state equations',statement=r'Determine the constraints and calculational recursions for primary-field matrix elements from Virasoro covariance and specified null states, distinguishing the additional channel and three-point data needed to obtain a full correlation function.',occurrences=['1982-573.4'],status=r'As of November 2026, the BPZ program, received in November 1983 and published in 1984, gives conformal blocks and degenerate-field differential equations. The notebook data alone do not determine structure constants or a unique full conformal theory; this is an underdetermination, not a newly posed open classification problem.',status_date='2026-11',refs=['belavinpolyakovzamolodchikov1984infinite']))
problems.append(dict(id='GP-1982-09',title='The Virasoro representation content of the Liouville spectrum',statement=r'Relate the stationary spectrum of a quantized and renormalized Liouville field theory to highest-weight Virasoro representations. A precise modern version fixes \(0<\gamma<2\), \(\mu>0\), and the reflection-positive probabilistic Liouville Hamiltonian.',occurrences=['1982-573.14'],status=r'As of November 2026, the real-coupling implementation has a complete continuous spectral resolution with Virasoro descendant states, proved in the 2020 preprint of Guillarmou--Kupiainen--Rhodes--Vargas and published in 2024. This does not identify the unrenormalized notebook action at every \(D<26\) with that theorem.',status_date='2026-11',refs=['guillarmoukupiainenrhodesvargas2024bootstrap']))
corr('P001058','Opening Virasoro commutator',r'\((m-n)L_{n+m}+(c/12)\delta_{n+m,0}\mathbf1\).',r'In the stated highest-weight/primary convention use \((n-m)L_{n+m}+\frac{c}{12}(n^3-n)\delta_{n+m,0}\mathbf1\).',r'Scan checked: no cubic factor is present. The written central term violates antisymmetry even at \(n=m=0\); the neighboring primary formula fixes the compatible bracket sign.')
corr('P001058','Sentence after the primary commutator',r'The operator is uniquely determined by this formula.',r'The covariance law needs module channels and a normalization; it is unchanged by scalar multiplication.',r'Russian and scan explicitly say uniquely. Multiplying any solution by a scalar is an immediate counterexample to unrestricted uniqueness.')
corr('P001058','Neretin reducibility polynomial convention',r'Both factors begin with \(-\Delta\).',r'Retain this polynomial with \(h=-\Delta\) under \(\ell_n=-L_n\) for the reverse Witt bracket; translate coherently if the opening bracket and weight are renormalized.',r'Scan confirms both minus signs. At \((\alpha,\beta)=(1,2)\) it gives \(16\Delta^2-2(c-5)\Delta+c=0\), exactly the standard level-two condition with \(h=-\Delta\). The polynomial alone is not a faithful mathematical error.',type='ambiguity')
corr('P001058','First degeneration level',r'Degeneration occurs at level \(\alpha\beta\).',r'That condition supplies a singular-vector level; the first is the minimum of \(rs\) over all matching positive integer pairs.',r'Multiple reducibility conditions may hold simultaneously; Feigin--Fuchs determinant gives factors at every level and their multiplicities.',type='ambiguity')
corr('P001058','Equation (2), matter and ghost determinants',r'\((\det\Delta_g)^{-D/2}(\det\mathcal L)^{1/2}\), with no primes or cosmological weight.',r'Use positive operators \(-\Delta_g\) and \(P^*P\), primed determinants and explicit zero-mode/moduli factors; retain the cosmological weight.',r'The Laplacian definition on P001059 is nonpositive. Integration by parts gives \(\mathcal L=-P^*P/2\). The area exponential in equation (1) does not disappear under the Gaussian matter integration.')
corr('P001059','Equation (3), first derivative',r'\(\nabla_a^a\varepsilon_b\).',r'\(\nabla_a\varepsilon_b\), with the upper mark recorded as doubtful or cancelled.',r'The scan has an overwritten upper mark. The free indices of the symmetric trace-free derivative permit no extra upper \(a\).',type='ambiguity')
corr('P001059','Critical-dimension flat-metric statement',r'At \(D=26\) the answer equals that for \(g_{ab}=\delta_{ab}\).',r'The local matter-plus-ghost Weyl anomaly cancels; residual moduli, automorphisms and insertion integrations remain.',r'The scan states an unqualified equality. A general closed surface has no single global Euclidean metric; the local cancellation is the precise prior result.')
corr('P001059','Stress tensor after equation (6)',r'\(\varphi_a\varphi_b-\frac12\delta_{ab}|\nabla\varphi|^2+\alpha\varphi_{ab}-\beta\delta_{ab}|\nabla\varphi|^2\).',r'For \(V=m_0^2e^\varphi\), a consistent tensor is \(\varphi_a\varphi_b-\delta_{ab}(\frac12|\nabla\varphi|^2+V)+2(\delta_{ab}\Delta\varphi-\varphi_{ab})\).',r'Scan confirms a repeated gradient-square final term. The corrected divergence and trace vanish by \(\Delta\varphi=V\); the printed ansatz cannot have both properties for general solutions.')
corr('P001059','Holomorphic and antiholomorphic stress formulas',r'\(\varphi_z^2+2\varphi_{zz}\) and its barred counterpart.',r'\(\varphi_z^2-2\varphi_{zz}\) and its barred counterpart, for the recorded \(e^\varphi\) action.',r'The plus signs are clear in the scan. Using \(\varphi_{z\bar z}=m_0^2e^\varphi/4\), the printed \(\partial_{\bar z}T\) equals \(m_0^2e^\varphi\varphi_z\), generally nonzero.')
corr('P001059','Second holomorphic conservation equation',r'\(\partial_zT=0\).',r'\(\partial_z\bar T=0\).',r'The scan has no bar on this \(T\); it is an omission already in the notebook, not an English translation error.')
corr('P001059','Final Laurent series',r'\(T(z)=\sum z^{n-2}L_n\).',r'Use \(T(z)=\sum L_nz^{-n-2}\), or consistently relabel all notebook modes by \(n\mapsto-n\).',r'Scan confirms \(n-2\). Residue extraction in the same convention as the primary commutator uses \(L_n=(2\pi i)^{-1}\oint z^{n+1}T(z)\,dz\).',type='ambiguity')
audit.append('## Record 573\nActual pages1058--1059; both complete scans and Russian text read. Confirmed missing cubic central term, both minus-Delta factors, the strong uniqueness sentence, extra overwritten derivative index, gradient-square tensor term, two improvement plus signs, missing bar and reversed Laurent exponent. The mode/weight changes are treated as a consistent convention repair, not independent arbitrary sign choices. In particular the polynomial is correct with h=-Delta under ell_n=-L_n; it is not classified false. Full primary BPZ https://qft.itp.ac.ru/BPZ.pdf opened and Sections2--5 read, especially (2.18)--(2.27),(3.9)--(3.10),(4.5)--(4.18),(5.1)--(5.8); receipt22Nov1983 is not circulation before1Nov1982. Complete Liouville preprint https://arxiv.org/pdf/2005.11530 opened; Section2.3 Hamiltonian,2.4 complete continuous spectral resolution referencing Theorem6.25,2.5 analytic continuation, Theorem1.1 and Corollary1.2, Section8 checked. ArXiv abstract confirms first23May2020 and v2 11Nov2020. Journal2024 metadata is shared from winter_geometry institutional verification; direct DOI inaccessible in this shard. Theorem1.1 requires gamma in(0,2), alpha_i<Q, alpha1+alpha2>Q and alpha3+alpha4>Q for its uncomplicated four-point integral; no arbitrary-dimensional or arbitrary-coupling resolution claimed. GP-1982-03 reused; GP08 is the specified correlator/constraint program and GP09 the spectrum relation. Earlier index has no exact Liouville-CFT or Virasoro problem before this year. Own checks: scalar-rescaling failure of uniqueness; level1/2 Gram matrices and Kac polynomial; stress divergence, trace and complex derivative; ghost adjoint sign. Formal integral and all-string statement remain distinct from constructed LCFT. No new class C/D added.')
